1
0:00:00,400 --> 0:00:04,390
So, I asked an AI shopping assistant for a purple table runner.

2
0:00:04,270 --> 0:00:04,760
Twice.

3
0:00:06,620 --> 0:00:11,130
On the left, it only sees the listings, and it goes with Violet and Vine.

4
0:00:11,010 --> 0:00:12,750
Cheaper, and fast shipping.

5
0:00:13,100 --> 0:00:16,760
On the right, it can check each shop's track record first.

6
0:00:16,640 --> 0:00:17,670
It picks Lydia.

7
0:00:18,020 --> 0:00:20,030
Here's what the left side missed:

8
0:00:19,910 --> 0:00:25,070
Violet and Vine lost its seal, because buyers were getting polyester, or nothing at all.

9
0:00:25,450 --> 0:00:26,790
And that wasn't a one-off.

10
0:00:27,030 --> 0:00:33,000
Across fifty requests, shops like that won thirty-four times without evidence, and zero times with it.

11
0:00:33,350 --> 0:00:36,390
Honest small shops went from nine wins to thirty-five.

12
0:00:36,740 --> 0:00:42,370
That matters, because a recent study found AI assistants pick the big chain about nine times out of ten.

13
0:00:43,000 --> 0:00:48,140
So how does it work? Real Deal Seal turns a small shop's PayPal history into a passport.

14
0:00:48,700 --> 0:00:51,880
It's not a score. It simply counts what happened:

15
0:00:51,760 --> 0:00:55,070
orders completed, shipments tracked, refunds given.

16
0:00:55,420 --> 0:00:58,010
And every fact tells you where it came from:

17
0:00:57,890 --> 0:01:01,500
PayPal's records, the shop itself, or simulated for this demo.

18
0:01:05,000 --> 0:01:08,030
The passport is signed, so anyone can check it.

19
0:01:07,910 --> 0:01:09,200
This one's genuine.

20
0:01:10,600 --> 0:01:14,450
Now watch. I change one number, and the check fails.

21
0:01:15,190 --> 0:01:18,690
This badge? We never signed it. It's a forgery.

22
0:01:19,040 --> 0:01:22,450
And if we withdraw a seal, the old badge stops working.

23
0:01:22,800 --> 0:01:25,670
All of this is plain code. No AI gets a vote.

24
0:01:26,200 --> 0:01:27,870
Now let's actually buy it.

25
0:01:27,750 --> 0:01:32,110
I ask the assistant, and it opens a PayPal checkout for Lydia's runner.

26
0:01:32,460 --> 0:01:34,170
But it can't pay for me.

27
0:01:34,050 --> 0:01:36,880
Only I can approve, on PayPal's own page.

28
0:01:37,230 --> 0:01:42,730
Then our server checks with PayPal that the exact amount was captured, and only then is it marked paid.

29
0:01:43,080 --> 0:01:46,280
It's the PayPal sandbox, so test money only.

30
0:01:46,160 --> 0:01:50,210
And a shop that lost its seal? Checkout is switched off.

31
0:01:52,000 --> 0:01:53,840
Shop owners get their own studio.

32
0:01:55,800 --> 0:01:57,680
Martha is missing four rules.

33
0:01:57,560 --> 0:02:02,640
One click, and she sees the exact orders behind the number, right here in AG Grid.

34
0:02:05,400 --> 0:02:10,650
So she adds a tracking number, and her count goes up by one, with a before and after on the record.

35
0:02:12,800 --> 0:02:15,050
We didn't just build it. We tested it:

36
0:02:14,930 --> 0:02:20,370
fifty requests and eleven attacks, written before any run, on two models, three full runs each.

37
0:02:21,560 --> 0:02:25,570
Forged seals, edited seals, hidden instructions, even pressure.

38
0:02:25,920 --> 0:02:30,190
And every run is published in an AG Grid you can search, sort and export.

39
0:02:30,070 --> 0:02:34,570
All six hundred picks, including the ones we re-ran after fixing our own bug.

40
0:02:35,100 --> 0:02:36,030
Under the hood:

41
0:02:35,910 --> 0:02:40,840
eight agent tools over MCP, and a data platform that checks every promise on every build.

42
0:02:45,000 --> 0:02:51,650
For PayPal, payment history becomes a reason for shops to stay, and a reason for AI agents to check out with PayPal.

43
0:02:52,080 --> 0:02:54,770
Real Deal Seal. Proof beats brand.

